fix(sync): one unreadable row must not abort the page it is on

`testOneBadItemDoesNotAbortTheSyncPass` says what the engine promises: three
items, one of them malformed, leaves `invalid: 1, skipped: 2, found: 3`. It was
failing because the whole synchronisation threw.

The per-item boundary that keeps that promise is still there and still correct —
it catches, records the failure to the dead-letter service, and carries on. What
changed is that the origin-id resolution moved OUT of it. `indexContractsByOrigin`
batches the contract lookup for the whole page, and to build its key list it maps
`getOriginId()` over every item BEFORE the loop starts. `getOriginId()` throws for
an item with no id. So one malformed row now killed the page from outside the
guard that exists to contain exactly that.

Batching the lookup is worth keeping; it is a real saving. Skipping an unreadable
row in the pre-pass keeps both, because the index is an OPTIMISATION rather than
a source of truth: an item missing from it resolves its own contract in the loop
exactly as it did before the batch existed, and an item whose origin id cannot be
read fails THERE, on its own, and is dead-lettered with a message naming the item
instead of taking down everything around it.

⚠️ The catch is written `\Throwable`, fully qualified, and that is load-bearing.
This file imports `Exception` and not `Throwable`, so a bare `Throwable` resolves
to `OCA\OpenConnector\Service\Throwable` — a class that does not exist. The catch
compiles, matches nothing, and the exception sails through as if it were never
written. Measured: with the bare name the test still failed, identically.

Full suite: 80 broken before, 77 after. Three fixed by name — this test plus both
SynchronizationScalarItemCoercion cases, which shared the cause — and NONE newly
broken.

Six failures remain and are deliberately not touched here, because they are not
this bug. They encode the PRE-BATCHING WRITE PATTERN:

  EndoflifeDateSyncTest x2   spy on `saveObject`, production now uses `saveObjects`
  …OriginIdMatchingTest x4   expect two writes ("identity, then outcomes"), one now

Both follow from the perf change deliberately, so rewriting the assertions to
match would be asserting the new pattern is correct — and that is a question for
whoever made the change. It is not obviously free: "identity, then outcomes"
exists so a crash between the two leaves a recoverable state, and collapsing it
to one write may preserve that or may not. Reported on ocon#1241.
